this prison security is carried out by  venezuela's national guard  a military unit we've not seen this at a  prison before  we've been stopped from entering here  for days the prison service granted sky  news unprecedented access to its prison  system  but the local authorities insist no  access will be granted here  despite orders from on high  eventually they said they'd change their  minds and would let us in  very soon it became clear to us it  wasn't their decision to make  here it is the prisoners who are in  charge  this is like no prison i've ever seen  before it's more like a town surrounded  by a wall  the prisoners watch on as we walk  through the corridors surrounded by  police  it feels like a poor village they've run  down cafes fast food takeaways  but it's all completely dilapidated  there are no lights in the darkened  accommodation areas  the prisoners hang out in the corridors  and we're told it's a no-go  area for us the prisoners won't allow it  we have 30 minutes and no questions  we've been told about the deal between  the prison guards and the prisoners is  that they basically leave each other  alone then everything stays cool  the prisoners misbehave then the water  the electricity and the food and family  visits go  but if the prisoners get angrier and  angrier then way  of fighting back is to set the prison on  fire  so there's a really tense truce the  whole time  we're joined throughout by a group of  men the man  with the bright yellow trainers we're  told is the prison boss he runs  everything  he and his own guards are all carrying  weapons they're tucked into their shores  the boss takes a dollar a week from the  inmates  there are five and a half thousand  prisoners here  the boss is clearing 5 500 us dollars  every week  inside they have weapons alcohol drugs  and women  this is a prison out of control  venezuela's prison infrastructure and  its legal system are crumbling  and this prison is falling apart  [Applause]  [Music]  [Ap